WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.112 --> 00:00:02.928
Welcome to Engineering
for a Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:03.196 --> 00:00:04.855
Hi! I am Riccardo,

00:00:05.546 --> 00:00:07.253
Developer Advocate in Google.

00:00:07.253 --> 00:00:09.588
I work with customers
on reliability topics.

00:00:10.198 --> 00:00:12.913
In this episode, we're going to explore
the Art of SLOs,

00:00:13.032 --> 00:00:15.473
and why it may be useful
for your organization.

00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.000
♪ [music] ♪

00:00:22.391 --> 00:00:24.653
Wait, what is Art of SLOs?

00:00:24.973 --> 00:00:27.927
It's a workshop that will teach you
how to choose SLIs and SLOs

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.289
for your workloads.

00:00:29.531 --> 00:00:31.615
But first, what are SLIs and SLOs?

00:00:32.454 --> 00:00:35.224
Well, you might've heard about
or even read the SRE books

00:00:35.556 --> 00:00:38.136
available for free on <i>sre.google/books.</i>

00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:40.562
Here you'll find plenty of information

00:00:40.562 --> 00:00:42.611
on how Google operates reliable services.

00:00:42.921 --> 00:00:44.785
One of the main takeaways of that

00:00:44.785 --> 00:00:47.288
is reliability is a key feature
of your service,

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:50.350
and that you should measure it
and set targets for it.

00:00:50.714 --> 00:00:52.532
At this point, you might ask yourself,

00:00:52.532 --> 00:00:53.797
"Okay, but how do I choose

00:00:53.797 --> 00:00:56.216
what reliability target metrics
and thresholds

00:00:56.216 --> 00:00:57.925
are appropriate for my application?

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.444
I mean, you don't want to be paged
every time a VM reboots, right?

00:01:01.791 --> 00:01:04.604
You only want to be paged
when you believe that your service

00:01:04.604 --> 00:01:06.617
is not meeting your user expectations.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:10.632
To get there, you need a way
to measure your service's ability

00:01:10.819 --> 00:01:12.084
to keep your users happy,

00:01:12.084 --> 00:01:14.878
in a way to set targets
for what's reliable enough.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.694
Let's delve into it.

00:01:17.926 --> 00:01:20.963
An SLI is a measure 
of how reliable a service is.

00:01:21.357 --> 00:01:23.509
For example, if your service
provides an API,

00:01:23.509 --> 00:01:25.648
you may choose Latency or Availability,

00:01:25.648 --> 00:01:27.567
but for a Data Processing pipeline,

00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:30.047
you may look more
at data Freshness or Throughput.

00:01:31.058 --> 00:01:35.493
An SLO describes when a service
is reliable enough to meet user needs.

00:01:36.188 --> 00:01:38.425
An Error Budget is just a complement
of the SLO,

00:01:38.842 --> 00:01:42.023
so if your team chooses that 99%
is Reliability target,

00:01:42.273 --> 00:01:44.021
the Error Budget will be 1%.

00:01:44.503 --> 00:01:47.652
That 1% is the acceptable level
of unreliability.

00:01:48.417 --> 00:01:51.780
You allocate the Error Budget
to maximize your development velocity,

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.197
while maintaining the reliability
of your service.

00:01:54.639 --> 00:01:57.541
Okay, but what is the right target,
what is enough?

00:01:58.633 --> 00:02:01.009
Setting the right SLO in practice
is really hard.

00:02:01.623 --> 00:02:04.055
This is why we have created
a workshop to teach you.

00:02:04.445 --> 00:02:06.351
We came up with a sample online game,

00:02:06.351 --> 00:02:08.493
played by millions
of users all over the world

00:02:08.493 --> 00:02:10.332
dividing to Vampire clans.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:13.711
We also have a Leader Board
to check if you are in the top ten.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.554
There are plenty
of business Critical User Journeys:

00:02:16.779 --> 00:02:20.611
Log in, Monetization, Battles, Profiles,
Leader Board Generation.

00:02:20.892 --> 00:02:23.006
All things your players care about.

00:02:23.628 --> 00:02:24.920
And how do you use this?

00:02:25.754 --> 00:02:28.783
Each Journey is a pragmatic
and self-contained exercise

00:02:29.044 --> 00:02:31.543
where you will need to find
what SLI you care about.

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:33.115
Let's say-- latency.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.310
Okay, but latency
between which two points in time?

00:02:36.611 --> 00:02:38.337
You need to break your journey down

00:02:38.337 --> 00:02:41.254
to the scope that you believe
is most important for your user.

00:02:41.756 --> 00:02:44.625
Your team initially will disagree
on which is more important,

00:02:44.625 --> 00:02:47.620
and the discussion that will come up,
until you reach consensus,

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:48.669
is crucial.

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:49.837
As you likely trade-off

00:02:49.837 --> 00:02:52.621
between business requirements
and engineering constraints.

00:02:53.388 --> 00:02:55.018
And now, where do we measure it?

00:02:55.018 --> 00:02:57.437
At the Load Balancer or on the client app?

00:02:57.772 --> 00:02:59.449
Your team will build up the muscles

00:02:59.449 --> 00:03:01.558
to determine the right SLOs 
fo your journey.

00:03:01.558 --> 00:03:03.664
And usually, after two
or three iterations,

00:03:03.855 --> 00:03:05.827
your team will get a lot better at this.

00:03:06.081 --> 00:03:08.291
"Sounds good, Riccardo,"
you might be thinking,

00:03:08.291 --> 00:03:10.557
"but how much does it cost?"

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:14.513
Nothing. It's all freely available
under Creative Commons license.

00:03:14.807 --> 00:03:16.921
You have slides and docs
for the facilitator

00:03:16.921 --> 00:03:18.581
who will moderate that workshop,

00:03:18.581 --> 00:03:21.495
and a Participant Handbook
that you can share with the team.

00:03:22.041 --> 00:03:25.818
Further, the workshop has materials
like Retrospective Exercise

00:03:25.818 --> 00:03:27.286
to refine an existing SLO.

00:03:28.154 --> 00:03:29.862
I hope I made you curious now.

00:03:29.862 --> 00:03:32.627
Feel free to check it out,
and let us know what you think.

00:03:32.981 --> 00:03:33.785
[ringing]

00:03:33.785 --> 00:03:35.328
Oh, sorry.

00:03:36.845 --> 00:03:37.812
[ringing]

00:03:38.878 --> 00:03:40.609
Sorry, folks. I've just been paged.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:41.904
We're burning Error Budget

00:03:41.904 --> 00:03:44.159
for our latency SLO
in the European region.

00:03:44.465 --> 00:03:47.462
I need to fix this ASAP to ensure
that we stay within our SLO.

00:03:48.201 --> 00:03:50.852
I knew I shouldn't have shot this video
while on call.

00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:53.268
Hah, wish me luck!

00:03:54.332 --> 00:03:56.431
♪ [outro music] ♪

